[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8856","issue":"2025-08-08T23:37:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS64","cccc":"KOHX","awipsid":"AFDOHX"}]
=================================================



978

FXUS64 KOHX 082337

AFDOHX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Nashville TN

637 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



...New UPDATE...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 636 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



- Low shower and storm chances (less than 20 percent) this

  weekend. Chances will increase to medium (40 to 60 percent) by

  next Tuesday and Wednesday. The severe storm risk remains very 

  low.

 

- Temperatures will be on the warm side of normal through the 

  next 7 days. Humidity will start out tolerable, but levels will 

  climb with heat index values topping out over 100 in some areas 

  next week. 



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 636 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



Scattered afternoon showers & thunderstorms are diminishing in 

coverage as peak daytime heating comes to an end. Radar does show 

a large outflow boundary making its way west, moving through 

Davidson & Williamson counties, but trends still suggest that the

window for rain today is closing. There's a low chance for fog 

overnight, especially across the Plateau which received up to 

around 1.5 inches of rain today. Any fog will dissipate early 

Saturday morning, and then a nice start to the weekend looks to be

on tap. Broad ridging building over the area will keep our highs 

in the mid-80s along the Plateau to the low-90s elsewhere. A 

mostly dry day also looks to be in store with very low rain 

chances (less than 20%) maintained in the forecast.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Rest of today through Sunday) 

Issued at 1130 AM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



We saw more coverage of showers and storms than expected yesterday

as a small scale boundary and MCV developed over the region. We 

do not think coverage will be a great this afternoon and evening, 

but the NBM looks too dry, especially compared to blended CAMs. 

So, we will go with 20 to 30 pops most areas and 40 percent along 

the Plateau. Scattered shower and isolated storms should die off 

pretty quickly with sunset. Late night fog will form in the usual 

favored locations and areas that receive downpours. 



For Saturday through Sunday, a broad upper ridge pattern will be

in place from the southwest states, across the Mid South and up 

to New England. Deep moisture will continue to lurk to our 

southeast from the Gulf coast to the Coastal Plains of the Carolinas.

Here in Middle TN, there will be enough low level moisture and

just enough diurnal instability for a low-end shower/storm

chance. Otherwise, temps will be a degree or 2 on the warm side 

of normal. Tolerable humidity levels will give peak heat index 

values in the mid 90s Saturday, but as dews creep upward, heat 

index values will approach 100 for some spots Sunday.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Sunday Night through next Friday) 

Issued at 1130 AM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



By Sunday night into Monday, upper level ridging will become

centered over the southeast with a surface high pressure along the

east coast. This will open Middle Tennessee up to more steamy

conditions with deep moisture advection from the gulf and from 

the blob of moisture that has been luring southeast of our area. 



Tuesday and Wednesday, a shortwave trough will move from the Ms 

Valley to Ohio Valley causing convergence and forcing to impinge 

on the northern side of the ridge. Pops will increase to at least

medium (40 to 60 percent) levels. The greater forcing combined 

with deeper moisture and instability will bring potential for

diurnally driven strong thunderstorms with local heavy downpours 

and gusty winds. We do not see indications for widespread severe

wx. Some spots will have downpours, but the lagre-scale rainfall

forecasts are not impressive, so we may see more areas start to 

slip into drought conditions.



Late net week, rain chances are expected to drop back to low-end

as upper level high pressure strengthens. It will be warmer than

normal and humid, so heat index values will climb over 100 each 

day for some areas west of the Plateau. Right now, we hope to 

stay below Heat Advisory level, but it may get close late next 

week.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 626 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



