987

FXUS64 KFWD 082338

AFDFWD



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Fort Worth TX

638 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



...New Aviation, Short Term... 



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A Heat Advisory remains in effect for most of North Texas and

  East Texas through Saturday afternoon.



- Slightly lower temperatures and low rain chances are expected

  over the weekend through next week.

 

&&



.SHORT TERM... /NEW/

Update:



No major changes needed to the short term forecast other than to

lower high temperatures a couple of degrees for Saturday along

with dewpoints. Heat index values will remain near or slightly

above 105 degrees.



Dunn



Previous Discussion:

/Through Saturday Afternoon/



Hot, humid, and quiet conditions will persist across North and 

Central Texas into the weekend, as the expansive upper ridge 

centered over New Mexico continues to dominate the synoptic 

pattern. Mid-level heights near 594 dam and a deeply mixed 

boundary layer will support highs in the upper 90s up to 105 

degrees. The most oppressive heat will be focused east of the I-35

corridor, where surface dewpoints in the lower to mid 70s will 

yield heat index values in the 105-110 degree range. Overnight 

lows will remain uncomfortably warm in the mid to upper 70s due to

elevated dewpoints and residual boundary layer warmth, offering 

little relief following the daytime heat. Persistent southerly 

winds will continue to transport Gulf moisture northward tonight, 

setting us up for another hot and humid day tomorrow.



On Saturday, the upper ridge is expected to weaken slightly as 

mid-level heights begin to fall in response to the weak inverted 

trough shifting westward along the Texas Gulf Coast and a 

deepening trough over the northern Plains. The net result is a 

subtle reduction in daytime highs, particularly across parts of 

Central Texas. East of the I-35 corridor, temperatures should be a

few degrees lower than what we see today, but with dewpoints 

remaining in the lower to mid 70s heat index values will again 

climb into the 105-110 degree range. Farther west, lower humidity 

will limit heat indices somewhat, but actual air temperatures may

still reach as high as 103 degrees in isolated locations. 

Therefore, the Heat Advisory that was set to expire later this 

evening has been extended (in its current configuration) through 7

PM CDT Saturday.



Despite the ridge's subtle weakening, strong subsidence will 

still preclude meaningful precipitation chances for most of the 

area. Some weak ascent along a diffuse seabreeze boundary may be 

sufficient to support a few isolated showers or storms to our 

southeast. However, it remains unlikely that any of this activity 

will reach the Brazos Valley before dissipating.



12



&&



.LONG TERM... /Issued 149 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025/

/Saturday Night and Beyond/



The upper ridge will continue its gradual westward shift late 

weekend into early next week as troughing amplifies over the Great

Lakes/Northeast and the previously mentioned inverted upper 

trough drifts nearby. This more trough dominated pattern will 

provide at least minor relief from the heat with highs gradually 

settling into the lower to mid 90s by midweek. Doesn't sound like

much of an improvement from current conditions, but these 

temperatures are actually below-normal for this time of the year. 

You have to take your wins where you can get them! The latest NBM

and ensemble guidance continues to support at least a low 

(20-30%) chance for daily seabreeze showers and thunderstorms

beginning Sunday, primarily across the Brazos Valley and adjacent

portions of Central Texas.



12



&&



.AVIATION... /NEW/

/00Z TAFs/



VFR will prevail through the period with south winds 10-15 kt. No

significant aviation concerns are expected through the period.



Dunn



&&



.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...

Spotter activation is not expected at this time.



&&



