2025-08-08T23-43-54-636Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9094","issue":"2025-08-08T23:43:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS64","cccc":"KHUN","awipsid":"AFDHUN"}]
=================================================



218

FXUS64 KHUN 082343

AFDHUN



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Huntsville AL

643 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



...New AVIATION...



.NEAR TERM...

(Rest of Today and Tonight)

Issued at 248 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



Mainly only showers showing up on radar though affiliated with 

that at this time, except in western Tennessee. Satellite imagery 

hints at a couple of weak trough axes between central Kentucky 

and northern Alabama/Georgia moving slowly south or southeastward 

the remainder of the afternoon. Expect additional scattered 

showers and thunderstorms to develop later this afternoon as these

trough axes push south into an unstable airmass. 



PWATS are between 1.5 and 1.8 inches, so plenty of moisture in 

the atmoshperic column to produce heavy downpours. Decent low 

level lapse rates (7.5 to 8.5 degrees Celsius/km), 2000 to 3000 

J/KG of SBCAPE, and DCAPE between 800 and 950 J/KG is seen in 

meso-analysis. Thus, a few storms could be strong producing gusty 

winds, very heavy downpours, and frequent lightning. Shear though 

remains weak, so no organized severe weather is expected. Expect 

the convection to dissipate early this evening. We may have to 

watch for more fog development, increasing winds towards daybreak 

may keep that at bay. 



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Saturday through Monday)

Issued at 248 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



Guidance continues to suggest that building upper level 

subsidence will help to really suppress convection and shower 

activity on Saturday. Temperatures should warm a tad into the 

lower 90s again near and west of the I-65 corridor. Further east 

and southeast bit more cloud cover could help to keep temperatures

in the 85 to 90 degree range. Some increased low level moisture 

advection should help to keep lows a bit warmer Saturday night, 

only dropping into the upper 60s to lower 70s. Some patchy fog may

occur, but winds may be just high enough to keep it at bay except

in sheltered valley locations. 



During the day on Sunday, models continue to show a weak upper low

off the Atlantic coast moving east or northeast. This will pull a

weak boundary into southern or central Alabama. The forcing and

instability in place could lend itself to a few showers or

thunderstorms (20-30%). A storm or two could be strong, but most

activity will likely remain near and south of the Tennessee River. 

Highs on Sunday look the warmest. A 94 or 95 degree high in NW AL

is possible. Heat index values will climb into the upper 90s to 

around 100 degrees. The diurnal shower and thunderstorm activity 

should dissipate quickly with the loss of daytime heating. Again 

slightly stronger winds overnight should keep fog at bay, except 

in sheltered valley locations. 



The boundary to our south, meanders slightly further northward 

Sunday night into Monday. It should allow for scattered shower and

thunderstorm activity to edge a bit further north. Scattered

activity (30-45%) should encompass both northern Alabama and

southern middle Tennessee as a result. A few strong storms could

occur with DCAPE values and decent lapse rates. 



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Monday night through Friday)

Issued at 248 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



Again, expect most activity to dissipate in the evening hours. 

Fog may become a bit more prevalent Monday night. 



A stronger trough axis and associated front moves toward the

Tennessee valley towards the middle of the week. This should

continue to increase shower and thunderstorm coverage through 

Thursday or Friday as the front moves closer and the front over 

central Alabama lifts further north into SW Tennessee and northern

Alabama. We may have to watch out for some flash flooding and 

flooding concerns towards the middle and end of next week. Some 

strong storms look possible, but shear doesn't increase enough to 

highlight an organized severe threat in the afternoon and evening.



It will be cooler with increasing cloud cover and especially

shower and thunderstorm chances Tuesday into Thursday. Highs will

likely be in the 85 to 90 degree range in most locations, despite

some warmer 925 mb temperatures shown in guidance. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 643 PM CDT Fri Aug 8 2025



VFR conditions will continue through the TAF period with only a

few light showers left on radar this evening. Chances for rain and

storms through tomorrow are too low to include in the TAFs at 

this point, and drier air in place should keep fog concerns mostly

at bay overnight. 



&&



.HUN WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

AL...None.

TN...None.

&&



$$



NEAR TERM...KTW

SHORT TERM....KTW

LONG TERM....KTW

AVIATION...25



